# Accepted residuals — BINDER-POLICY.md §6 and §6a.
#
# A residual is accepted by being NAMED, never by being hidden. Each line below
# records a gate hit that is data rather than the thing the rule exists to stop,
# and says what the data is. Format: <card>|<substring of the gate's message>
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 03_mqtt_contract.md — caps-runs 4.8/1k against a 4.0 ceiling
#
# Four runs. One is the binder's own header; the other three are LITERAL VALUES
# CARRIED ON THE WIRE, quoted so a consumer can match them exactly:
#
#   PHOENIX NEST LLC   the front-matter line every card in the estate carries
#   UNDER ZERO         sensor value: below the live zero, still measuring
#   XMTR FAULT         sensor value: at or above the fault threshold
#   BAD STATE          sensor value: a corrupted record, never a live reading
#
# The three sensor values are produced verbatim by loopStateName() in
# include/loop_scaling.h and appear in the weight, telemetry and status
# payloads. A consumer matches them as strings. Re-casing them to satisfy the
# gate would make the card disagree with the wire, which is the same failure
# §6 describes for panel legends: the card must read the way the thing in front
# of the reader reads.
#
# "NO SENSOR" and "SATURATED" are also sensor values and also appear on this
# card. The gate does not count them -- its pattern needs three or more capitals
# in the first word of a run -- which is a further sign the count tracks letter
# shapes rather than shouting.
03_mqtt_contract.md|caps-runs
